{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"Verified in SCOPe release 2.08: ferritins (family a.25.1.1 'Ferritin', sunid 47241), ribonucleotide reductase-like proteins (family a.25.1.2 'Ribonucleotide reductase-like', sunid 47253, with the R2 protein node 47257 beneath it), and manganese catalases (family a.25.1.3 'Manganese catalase (T-catalase)', sunid 100951) all sit in superfamily a.25.1 'Ferritin-like' (sunid 47240), within fold a.25 and class a (all alpha). The superfamily annotation says it 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle'. Homology is implied at superfamily and family level with no warnings for any of the three nodes.

Interpretation: SCOPe supports a shared superfamily-level relationship (probable common ancestry of the conserved bimetal-binding four-helix bundle) among these three families. It does not place them in a closer family-level relationship, because they are three distinct families within a.25.1. The family split is where the chemistries are classified apart, and SCOPe itself says nothing about iron storage, ferroxidation, radical generation, or manganese-based catalysis. Those chemistry descriptions are background knowledge, not SCOPe-verified, and they must not be transferred between families. Shared superfamily membership is not a claim of shared function, so the functional divergence is an expected interpretation of a superfamily-level relationship, not something SCOPe establishes.

Limits: SCOPe makes no negative claim here; the different chemistries do not make these proteins nonhomologous, analogous, or convergent, and SCOPe does not assert any of those. The name 'ribonucleotide reductase' is ambiguous in SCOPe: the same search returned unrelated RNR subunit folds (a.98.1 N-terminal R1, c.7.1.x R1 C-terminal, NRDD, and class II B12-dependent, plus j.62.1 and k.24.1), which must not be merged with the R2-like a.25.1.2 family. The Manganese catalase family record states it was 'automatically mapped to Pfam 05067', so that placement is partly automated. The ferritin and RNR-like family records have no Pfam or UniProt xrefs. The a.25.1.0 'automated matches' family also exists and was not evaluated. No sequence or FATCAT hits, alignments, or residue-level mapping were retrieved in this session, so any specific ancestry, mechanism, or catalytic-residue claim needs sequence or structure alignment, or a literature source, that was not checked here.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ferritin&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=manganese+catalase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ribonucleotide+reductase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/children?limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/homology","scop-homology/SKILL.md"]}]}
